speeches.en:
Thank you, Honourable Speaker. I too read the whole of the Maldives 2.0 Digital Transformation Bill, Honourable Speaker. However, in reality, some of the things said here up to today, up to this moment, are not in the bill. Talk of corruption, talk of business expanding. I have no hope, Honourable Speaker, that much will become digital through this, or that it will happen. That is because up to today many bills related to cyber security, protecting public information and such matters have come to this place. However, what came to my mind is that it would have been very complete if this bill had been designed with a view to how the Maldives would look while they themselves are in opposition, whether at some point in time, or while in opposition 5 years from now. There are good things in this too. However, in committee and here as well, since this is a very technical bill, there are very skilled, senior Maldivians educated in this field who love the country, and very talented young people who belong to no particular political ideology. It is my sincere request that the committee act in a way that gives them good time, so that the thinking of them, or of those working in this field, and of young people who hope for a digital Maldives, can be brought into this bill. I too have shared this bill with some friends I know for their comments. But Honourable Speaker, my greatest concern is that in this bill too, full control lies with a minister whom we accuse of having committed very serious wrongdoing even today. It is also the minister who is accountable to Parliament under this. We have sent very many questions to ministers. The Rules of Procedure of Parliament set out particular principles and time limits by which they must be accountable. Honourable Speaker;

the Honourable Speaker does not work on that either. The ministers do not work on that either. So this bill says the minister will be accountable to Parliament. So at that point I lost the hope I had in this bill. I cannot see, and especially among the ministers we now have, I cannot imagine that the minister who gets this mandate will be accountable to this Parliament. Because it has not happened so far, Honourable Speaker. Therefore, for such an important bill, whatever government is in power, the institution being created, and this digital, Commissioner for Digital Technology, this person too, must not be someone under the politics of that government or of any institution in which they hold any power; Honourable Speaker, I do not believe that if it is someone under the control of a political ideology, whatever government is in power, any of the benefit Maldivians hope for under this Digital Transformation Bill, or for a digital Maldives, will be realised. Honourable Speaker; this is not something happening today. As technology advanced, the Maldives too kept pace with these things, Honourable Speaker. Among these, Vangov and eFaas and such have already been made. However, even now the eFaas service, or good service to them, is provided only during government official hours. With very many services tied to eFaas, I do not believe the eFaas service should now be available only during official hours. I believe the government can find a way to provide this service on a broader basis. That would be an additional push towards making eFaas something familiar or friendly to Maldivians. In addition, this bill in reality takes 6 months to start implementing all its chapters at once. This too is a concern, Honourable Speaker. Whether 6 months is a good time for something wanted done this quickly is something important for us to consider at committee stage. And going to committee, to put it briefly, the bill also makes it mandatory to implement all the chapters in it within about a year. So, in addition to this, I also want to note that some things in this do not match the government's actions today, the purpose of this bill and the government's actions. Honourable Speaker; just a small example. This government coming in and acting to give full control of this Xpat system to a foreign party in a way that leaves the Maldives with no control, beginning to act on the minister's initiative, is contrary to the purpose of this bill. Therefore, that information and these matters must remain within the Maldives' sovereignty; sovereign cloud and such

many things must be established within this, must be ensured. While it includes matters that the institution created under this must necessarily ensure and give Maldivians assurance on, I want to note that some of the things this government has done by the time it has reached this point are contrary to the purpose of this bill. There are still very important matters. Therefore, so that I can speak further on some matters in this bill going forward, I cut this opportunity short for now. Thank you, Honourable Speaker.
